From bfd5d892cedf95f26a75bc989dd12250e847838a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mshresponse <9147626+mshresponse@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 03:16:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build the full edit / gap / orphan matrix for stationary and
 fleet

Reframes the package around the job an ESG team actually has during
implementation: getting data in and correcting it, without a Salesforce admin
and without another CSV round trip.

Three report type shapes, each doing something the others cannot:

  with (inner join)          every row resolves to a real child record, so
                             child columns are editable inline
  with/without (outer join)  finds records that need creating; deliberately
                             NOT editable, because inline editing cannot
                             create a record
  standalone                 carries the parent lookup Id columns so orphaned
                             children can be filtered out and reassigned

Outer joins are the durable part: "A with or without B" is only expressible in
a custom report type, so no standard report type Salesforce ships can replace
the gap-finding half of this package.

16 report types, 16 reports and 2 dashboards, mirrored across the stationary
and fleet sides. Edit reports are tabular with the editable fields as columns
and nothing important used as a grouping, since grouping fields cannot be
edited inline. Every report description names the Setup prerequisite, because
whoever installs this from GitHub has nobody to ask.

Two findings worth recording. Energy use records hang off two parents, either
a footprint or an asset, so an orphan requires both lookups blank; filtering
on one would miss half of them. And both asset objects are self-hierarchical
via ChildAssets and ChildVehicleAssets.

Every object, field, relationship and lookup name in this commit was read from
a describe against a Net Zero Cloud org at API 67.0 and is checked by a
verification pass over the whole tree. Nothing is inferred from documentation
or naming convention.

